"default_on": false,
"decisions": {
 "heard_by_default": "Every general entry here and every AcademicPass rule: their contexts (Eq. before a number, (ed.), ibid., viz., fl. before a year, numbered citations in brackets) only occur in academic writing. There is no Settings switch for packs yet (FIN-890 follow-up), and enabledPacks is empty by default, so the pack_only entries below are not heard until the academic pack is switched on (defaults write enabledPacks, or --packs academic in the test modes).",
 "field_only": ["OR: odds ratio, read O-R (pack_only). Everyone else hears the word 'or', or Oregon after a city (Core)."],
 "calls": [
  "viz. reads 'namely', as it is traditionally read aloud; Merriam-Webster gives no spoken form for the abbreviation.",
  "ibid. is IB-id (Merriam-Webster \\ˈi-bəd\\); op. cit. and loc. cit. are said as written (op SIT, loh SIT), not expanded to the Latin.",
  "Citation brackets with numbers ([12], [3–5], [1, 4; 9]) are silent; '[sic]' is read. A bracket with a 0 in it, after a maths sign or after 'x in', is an interval or vector and stays.",
  "BCE, CE, BC and AD, CI, SD, R², n = 30, p < .05 and et al. were already right (letters, 'R squared', 'n equals thirty'); they get tests, not entries.",
  "ed. is only expanded in an academic context: '(ed.)', '3rd ed.', 'ed. by', 'Smith, J., ed. 2020', ', ed. Jane Smith'. A bare 'Ed.' is the name and 'special ed.' the word. trans. and repr. get the same treatment.",
  "The Greek letters the stack couldn't say (eta, kappa, rho, chi, psi and the rest, and most capitals) are general entries: χ² was '[unknown] squared'. Names from the gold dictionary's own words (zeta, eta, kappa...). χ² and η² are keys of their own, as R² is in the tech list."
 ]
},
